DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ccttllaaddmm utility is designed to provide a way to access and control the
     CAM Target Layer (CTL).  It provides a way to send SCSI commands to the
     CTL layer, and also provides some meta-commands that utilize SCSI com‐
     mands.  (For instance, the lluunnlliisstt command is implemented using the SCSI
     REPORT LUNS and INQUIRY commands.)

     The ccttllaaddmm utility has a number of primary functions, many of which re‐
     quire a device identifier.  The device identifier takes the following
     form:

     lun             Specify the LUN number to operate on.
     Many of the primary functions of the ccttllaaddmm utility take the following
     optional arguments:

     --CC _r_e_t_r_i_e_s  Specify the number of times to retry a command in the event
                 of failure.

     --DD _d_e_v_i_c_e   Specify the device to open.  This allows opening a device
                 other than the default device, _/_d_e_v_/_c_a_m_/_c_t_l, to be opened for
                 sending commands.

     --II _i_d       Specify the initiator number to use.  By default, ccttllaaddmm will
                 use 7 as the initiator number.

     Primary commands:

     ttuurr          Send the SCSI TEST UNIT READY command to the device and re‐
                  port whether or not it is ready.

     iinnqquuiirryy      Send the SCSI INQUIRY command to the device and display some
                  of the returned inquiry data.

     rreeqqsseennssee     Send the SCSI REQUEST SENSE command to the device and dis‐
                  play the returned sense information.

     rreeppoorrttlluunnss   Send the SCSI REPORT LUNS command to the device and display
                  supported LUNs.

     rreeaadd         Send a SCSI READ command to the device, and write the re‐
                  quested data to a file or stdout.

                  --ll _l_b_a        Specify the starting Logical Block Address for
                                the READ.  This can be specified in decimal,
                                octal (starting with 0), hexadecimal (starting
                                with 0x) or any other base supported by
                                strtoull(3).

                  --dd _d_a_t_a_l_e_n    Specify the length, in 512 byte blocks, of the
                                READ request.

                  --ff _f_i_l_e       Specify the destination for the data read by
                                the READ command.  Either a filename or ‘-’
                                for stdout may be specified.

                  --cc _c_d_b_s_i_z_e    Specify the minimum SCSI CDB (Command Data
                                Block) size to be used for the READ request.
                                Allowable values are 6, 10, 12 and 16.  De‐
                                pending upon the LBA and amount of data re‐
                                quested, a larger CDB size may be used to sat‐
                                isfy the request.  (e.g., for LBAs above
                                0xffffffff, READ(16) must be used to satisfy
                                the request.)

                  --bb _b_l_o_c_k_s_i_z_e  Specify the blocksize of the underlying SCSI
                                device, so the transfer length can be calcu‐
                                lated accurately.  The blocksize can be ob‐
                                tained via the SCSI READ CAPACITY command.

                  --NN            Do not copy data to ccttllaaddmm from the kernel
                                when doing a read, just execute the command
                                without copying data.  This is to be used for
                                performance testing.

     wwrriittee        Read data from a file or stdin, and write the data to the
                  device using the SCSI WRITE command.

                  --ll _l_b_a        Specify the starting Logical Block Address for
                                the WRITE.  This can be specified in decimal,
                                octal (starting with 0), hexadecimal (starting
                                with 0x) or any other base supported by
                                strtoull(3).

                  --dd _a_t_a_l_e_n     Specify the length, in 512 byte blocks, of the
                                WRITE request.

                  --ff _f_i_l_e       Specify the source for the data to be written
                                by the WRITE command.  Either a filename or
                                ‘-’ for stdin may be specified.

                  --cc _c_d_b_s_i_z_e    Specify the minimum SCSI CDB (Command Data
                                Block) size to be used for the READ request.
                                Allowable values are 6, 10, 12 and 16.  De‐
                                pending upon the LBA and amount of data re‐
                                quested, a larger CDB size may be used to sat‐
                                isfy the request.  (e.g., for LBAs above
                                0xffffffff, READ(16) must be used to satisfy
                                the request.)

                  --bb _b_l_o_c_k_s_i_z_e  Specify the blocksize of the underlying SCSI
                                device, so the transfer length can be calcu‐
                                lated accurately.  The blocksize can be ob‐
                                tained via the SCSI READ CAPACITY command.

                  --NN            Do not copy data to ccttllaaddmm to the kernel when
                                doing a write, just execute the command with‐
                                out copying data.  This is to be used for per‐
                                formance testing.

     rreeaaddccaapp      Send the SCSI READ CAPACITY command to the device and dis‐
                  play the device size and device block size.  By default,
                  READ CAPACITY(10) is used.  If the device returns a maximum
                  LBA of 0xffffffff, however, ccttllaaddmm will automatically issue
                  a READ CAPACITY(16), which is implemented as a service ac‐
                  tion of the SERVICE ACTION IN(16) opcode.  The user can
                  specify the minimum CDB size with the --cc argument.  Valid
                  values for the --cc option are 10 and 16.  If a 10 byte CDB is
                  specified, the request will be automatically reissued with a
                  16 byte CDB if the maximum LBA returned is 0xffffffff.

     mmooddeesseennssee    Send a SCSI MODE SENSE command to the device, and display
                  the requested mode page(s) or page list.

                  --mm _p_a_g_e     Specify the mode page to display.  This option
                              and the --ll option are mutually exclusive.  One
                              of the two must be specified, though.  Mode page
                              numbers may be specified in decimal or hexadeci‐
                              mal.

                  --ll          Request that the list of mode pages supported by
                              the device be returned.  This option and the --mm
                              option are mutually exclusive.  One of the two
                              must be specified, though.

                  --PP _p_c       Specify the mode page control value.  Possible
                              values are:
                              0   Current values.
                              1   Changeable value bitmask.
                              2   Default values.
                              3   Saved values.

                  --dd          Disable block descriptors when sending the mode
                              sense request.

                  --SS _s_u_b_p_a_g_e  Specify the subpage used with the mode sense re‐
                              quest.

                  --cc _c_d_b_s_i_z_e  Specify the CDB size used for the mode sense re‐
                              quest.  Supported values are 6 and 10.

     ssttaarrtt        Send the SCSI START STOP UNIT command to the specified LUN
                  with the start bit set.

                  --ii    Set the immediate bit in the CDB.  Note that CTL does
                        not support the immediate bit, so this is primarily
                        useful for making sure that CTL returns the proper er‐
                        ror.

     ssttoopp         Send the SCSI START STOP UNIT command to the specified LUN
                  with the start bit cleared.  We use an ordered tag to stop
                  the LUN, so we can guarantee that all pending I/O executes
                  before it is stopped.  (CTL guarantees this anyway, but
                  ccttllaaddmm sends an ordered tag for completeness.)

                  --ii    Set the immediate bit in the CDB.  Note that CTL does
                        not support the immediate bit, so this is primarily
                        useful for making sure that CTL returns the proper er‐
                        ror.

     ssyynnccccaacchhee    Send the SCSI SYNCHRONIZE CACHE command to the device.  By
                  default, SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(10) is used.  If the specified
                  starting LBA is greater than 0xffffffff or the length is
                  greater than 0xffff, though, SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(16) will be
                  used.  The 16 byte command will also be used if the user
                  specifies a 16 byte CDB with the --cc argument.

                  --ll _l_b_a          Specify the starting LBA of the cache region
                                  to synchronize.  This option is a no-op for
                                  CTL.  If you send a SYNCHRONIZE CACHE com‐
                                  mand, it will sync the cache for the entire
                                  LUN.

                  --bb _b_l_o_c_k_c_o_u_n_t   Specify the length of the cache region to
                                  synchronize.  This option is a no-op for
                                  CTL.  If you send a SYNCHRONIZE CACHE com‐
                                  mand, it will sync the cache for the entire
                                  LUN.

                  --rr              Specify relative addressing for the starting
                                  LBA.  CTL does not support relative address‐
                                  ing, since it only works for linked com‐
                                  mands, and CTL does not support linked com‐
                                  mands.

                  --ii              Tell the target to return status immediately
                                  after issuing the SYNCHRONIZE CACHE command
                                  rather than waiting for the cache to finish
                                  syncing.  CTL does not support this bit.

                  --cc _c_d_b_s_i_z_e      Specify the minimum CDB size.  Valid values
                                  are 10 and 16 bytes.

     lluunnlliisstt      List all LUNs registered with CTL.  Because this command
                  uses the ioctl port, it will only work when the FETDs (Front
                  End Target Drivers) are enabled.  This command is the equiv‐
                  alent of doing a REPORT LUNS on one LUN and then an INQUIRY
                  on each LUN in the system.

     ddeellaayy        Delay commands at the given location.  There are two places
                  where commands may be delayed currently: before data is
                  transferred (“datamove”) and just prior to sending status to
                  the host (“done”).  One of the two must be supplied as an
                  argument to the --ll option.  The --tt option must also be spec‐
                  ified.

                  --ll _d_e_l_a_y_l_o_c   Delay command(s) at the specified location.
                                This can either be at the data movement stage
                                (datamove) or prior to command completion
                                (done).

                  --tt _d_e_l_a_y_t_i_m_e  Delay command(s) for the specified number of
                                seconds.  This must be specified.  If set to
                                0, it will clear out any previously set delay
                                for this particular location (datamove or
                                done).

                  --TT _d_e_l_a_y_t_y_p_e  Specify the delay type.  By default, the ddeellaayy
                                option will delay the next command sent to the
                                given LUN.  With the --TT _c_o_n_t option, every
                                command will be delayed by the specified pe‐
                                riod of time.  With the --TT _o_n_e_s_h_o_t the next
                                command sent to the given LUN will be delayed
                                and all subsequent commands will be completed
                                normally.  This is the default.

     iinnjjeecctt       Inject the specified type of error for the LUN specified,
                  when a command that matches the given pattern is seen.  The
                  sense data returned is in either fixed or descriptor format,
                  depending upon the status of the D_SENSE bit in the control
                  mode page (page 0xa) for the LUN.

                  Errors are only injected for commands that have not already
                  failed for other reasons.  By default, only the first com‐
                  mand matching the pattern specified is returned with the
                  supplied error.

                  If the --cc flag is specified, all commands matching the pat‐
                  tern will be returned with the specified error until the er‐
                  ror injection command is deleted with --dd flag.

                  --ii _a_c_t_i_o_n          Specify the error to return:

                                     aborted     Return the next matching com‐
                                                 mand on the specified LUN
                                                 with the sense key ABORTED
                                                 COMMAND (0x0b), and the
                                                 ASC/ASCQ 0x45,0x00 ("Select
                                                 or reselect failure").

                                     mediumerr   Return the next matching com‐
                                                 mand on the specified LUN
                                                 with the sense key MEDIUM ER‐
                                                 ROR (0x03) and the ASC/ASCQ
                                                 0x11,0x00 ("Unrecovered read
                                                 error") for reads, or
                                                 ASC/ASCQ 0x0c,0x02 ("Write
                                                 error - auto reallocation
                                                 failed") for write errors.

                                     ua          Return the next matching com‐
                                                 mand on the specified LUN
                                                 with the sense key UNIT AT‐
                                                 TENTION (0x06) and the
                                                 ASC/ASCQ 0x29,0x00 ("POWER
                                                 ON, RESET, OR BUS DEVICE RE‐
                                                 SET OCCURRED").

                                     custom      Return the next matching com‐
                                                 mand on the specified LUN
                                                 with the supplied sense data.
                                                 The --ss argument must be spec‐
                                                 ified.

                  --pp _p_a_t_t_e_r_n         Specify which commands should be returned
                                     with the given error.

                                     read        The error should apply to
                                                 READ(6), READ(10), READ(12),
                                                 READ(16), etc.

                                     write       The error should apply to
                                                 WRITE(6), WRITE(10),
                                                 WRITE(12), WRITE(16), WRITE
                                                 AND VERIFY(10), etc.

                                     rw          The error should apply to
                                                 both read and write type com‐
                                                 mands.

                                     readcap     The error should apply to
                                                 READ CAPACITY(10) and READ
                                                 CAPACITY(16) commands.

                                     tur         The error should apply to
                                                 TEST UNIT READY commands.

                                     any         The error should apply to any
                                                 command.

                  --rr _l_b_a_,_l_e_n         Specify the starting lba and length of
                                     the range of LBAs which should trigger an
                                     error.  This option is only applies when
                                     read and/or write patterns are specified.
                                     If used with other command types, the er‐
                                     ror will never be triggered.

                  --ss _l_e_n _f_m_t [_a_r_g_s]  Specify the sense data that is to be re‐
                                     turned for custom actions.  If the format
                                     is ‘-’, len bytes of sense data will be
                                     read from standard input and written to
                                     the sense buffer.  If len is longer than
                                     252 bytes (the maximum allowable SCSI
                                     sense data length), it will be truncated
                                     to that length.  The sense data format is
                                     described in cam_cdbparse(3).

                  --cc                 The error injection should be persistent,
                                     instead of happening once.  Persistent
                                     errors must be deleted with the --dd argu‐
                                     ment.

                  --dd _d_e_l_e_t_e___i_d       Delete the specified error injection se‐
                                     rial number.  The serial number is re‐
                                     turned when the error is injected.

     ppoorrtt         Perform one of several CTL frontend port operations.  Either
                  create a new frontend port (--cc), destroy a frontend port
                  (--rr), turn one or more frontends on or off (--oo _o_n_|_o_f_f), or
                  set the World Wide Node Name (--ww _w_w_n_n) or World Wide Port
                  Name (--WW _w_w_p_n) for a given port.  One of --cc, --rr, --oo, or --ww
